      academicExplanation: "Developing Benchmarks: IFIs need benchmarks for pricing goods/services and determining joint-venture profit-sharing ratios; such benchmarks differ across jurisdictions/sectors/subsectors and require deep study accounting for development level, supply/demand, and customer assets/liabilities - needed at both international and country levels (citing Abbas Mirakhor and Nadeem ul Haque's 1998 effort developing national participation paper (NPP) return-calculation indices as one such attempt). For now, conventional benchmarks are used almost everywhere; while permissible as a pricing tool for goods/usufruct, a benchmark reflecting fictitious assets (as in the conventional framework) will not help realize Islamic banking's socio-economic objectives - requiring long-term, sustained effort from economists, bankers, policymakers and Shari'ah scholars. Product Development - Financial Engineering: innovative products for cash management and financing various sectors (especially government/public sector) cannot be overemphasized, requiring mutual effort from economists, practitioners, Shari'ah scholars and regulators; the major challenge is ensuring Shari'ah compliance consistent with mainstream developed theory - any Shari'ah interpretation unacceptable to the scholarly majority would damage the industry's long-term image. Public finance product development, in collaboration with fiscal authorities, has much work remaining; Sukuk/securitization potential can help significantly. In genuine need cases, Shari'ah boards may offer well-defined, specified relaxations without compromising cardinal Shari'ah principles - but vague observations/edicts risk anarchy and loss of integrity for both the theory and the scholars themselves. Liquidity and Monetary Management: this area has seen insufficient work so far; a related issue is the availability of liquidity management instruments. Compared to conventional institutions, IFIs must place a GREATER portion of deposits with central banks as statutory liquidity reserves (SLR), earning NO RETURN on them - harming their competitiveness in offering depositors competitive returns. While some liquidity management instruments have developed, the modus operandi for using them in open market operations (OMOs) and repo transactions still needs to evolve. Establishing inter-bank Islamic money markets in jurisdictions with significant IFI presence is key for short-term liquidity/monetary management and Islamic finance promotion generally; demand-supply mismatches are normally covered via inter-bank transactions on the Mudarabah principle. Islamic banks can also invest with conventional banks offering Islamic banking business, provided the latter invests on a Shari'ah-compliant basis with Shari'ah-advisor-certified segregation of Riba-free and Riba-based deposits/financing. Banks may also securitize assets or manage funds via certificates of deposit/investment tradable in the secondary market (requiring cash/receivables components below 50% of the securitized pool/fund assets, ensuring a genuine secondary market for genuinely asset-based certificates). Regarding the central bank as lender of last resort (the conventional system's most strategic feature), the author proposes two Islamic-framework options: (1) a Mudarabah-based facility for a defined-extent liquidity crisis (e.g. 2-3 days), with a profit-sharing ratio (e.g. 50:50) on a daily-product basis, potentially shifting more favourably toward the central bank if the IFI cannot repay by the due date; (2) establishing a common pool at the central bank, funded by all IFIs contributing a specified deposit percentage, giving members the right to interest-free accommodation for up to (three) days during a defined liquidity crunch. Supply of Well-versed Human Resources: a major current bottleneck is the scarcity of trained human resources; though IFIs are intermediaries like conventional banks, Islamic bankers need a significantly different mindset and special orientation - all incumbents should be technically competent AND well aware of/committed to Islamic finance principles, since only committed, ethically-embedded, competent personnel can market products effectively and enhance the discipline's acceptability. Long-term growth requires an HR development framework, boosted R&D activity, and enhanced training/education facilities in collaboration with leading academic/research institutions - requiring joint effort from IFIs, Shari'ah scholars, central banks/regulators, universities, business schools and the student community, with universities recognizing the huge need for competent human resources, focusing on the philosophy AND practical operations of Islamic finance as it has evolved, avoiding unnecessary controversy.",
